{#- ACHILLES TOOL, v1 — English sibling of /members/aquiles/. Same 8 routines,
    same gated block, same library structure; only the copy changes.

    Original note follows.

    ACHILLES TOOL, v1 — built Aug 13, 2026 from Iván's Achilles source doc.
    Home doc: `achilles-brief.md`. Second artifact on the strength engine.

    FOUR questions → one of 8 routines, plus one gated block:
      location (mid-portion | insertional) × place (home | gym) × roller
      + an energy-storage block shown only if the athlete says the strength
        work is already pain-free.

    *** The location axis is the reason this tool is not a copy of the knee one.
    Insertional tendinopathy is made WORSE by dorsiflexion past neutral, which
    compresses the tendon against the calcaneus — and that is exactly what a
    heel drop off a step does at the bottom of every rep. So insertional gets:
    flat ground instead of a step, range capped at neutral, capped range on the
    gym machines, and NO end-range calf stretch at all. Do not "simplify" this
    away; following the source doc as written would have had insertional
    athletes doing 45 compressive reps a session. ***

    Frequency differs by place, on Iván's call: home = Alfredson (daily, twice
    daily), gym = HSR (3×/week), with a note saying they are two roads to the
    same place. -#}
{% raw %}
<div class="setup" id="setup">
  <div class="crumb-bar"><div class="wrap"><a href="/members/en/#biblioteca">Library</a> / Achilles</div></div>

  <div class="setup-head">
    <span class="label">Strength</span>
    <h1>Pain-free Achilles</h1>
    <p>Tendons don't heal with rest. They heal with well-dosed load. This is the loading programme — and the rules for knowing, each day, whether you overdid it.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="setup-warn">
    <strong>Two things before you start.</strong><br>
    <strong>1. If you felt a snap and you cannot push off or rise onto the toes of that foot, go to urgent care today</strong> — that is not tendinopathy. <br>
    <strong>2. This starts after a professional has seen you</strong> and ruled out a partial tear. With swelling, heat or redness, or if you have recently taken fluoroquinolone antibiotics, talk it through before loading the tendon. Write to me and we'll sort it out.
  </div>

  <div class="setup-q">
    <div class="setup-label">Where exactly does it hurt?</div>
    <div class="setup-opts" data-axis="site">
      <button type="button" class="setup-opt" data-val="mid" aria-pressed="true">Mid-portion<span>2-6 cm above the heel</span></button>
      <button type="button" class="setup-opt" data-val="ins" aria-pressed="false">Right at the bottom<span>where it meets the bone</span></button>
    </div>
    <p class="setup-hint"><strong>Press with two fingers and find where it jumps.</strong> This is not a detail: if the pain is low, at the insertion, dropping the heel below the step crushes the tendon against the bone on every single rep. It is the most common mistake in Achilles rehab, and the routine changes to avoid it.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="setup-q">
    <div class="setup-label">Where are you training?</div>
    <div class="setup-opts" data-axis="place">
      <button type="button" class="setup-opt" data-val="home" aria-pressed="true">At home<span>daily protocol</span></button>
      <button type="button" class="setup-opt" data-val="gym" aria-pressed="false">At the gym<span>heavy load, 3×/week</span></button>
    </div>
    <p class="setup-hint">Two roads to the same place. At home is the classic eccentric protocol: high frequency, little weight. At the gym is heavy slow resistance: three times a week, more weight, fewer sessions. The trials tie them; the gym one gets abandoned less often.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="setup-q">
    <div class="setup-label">Do you have a foam roller?</div>
    <div class="setup-opts" data-axis="roller">
      <button type="button" class="setup-opt" data-val="yes" aria-pressed="true">Yes</button>
      <button type="button" class="setup-opt" data-val="no" aria-pressed="false">No</button>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="setup-q">
    <div class="setup-label">Is the strength work already pain-free?</div>
    <div class="setup-opts" data-axis="stage">
      <button type="button" class="setup-opt" data-val="no" aria-pressed="true">Not yet</button>
      <button type="button" class="setup-opt" data-val="yes" aria-pressed="false">Yes, pain-free</button>
    </div>
    <p class="setup-hint">If you get through all the strength work with no discomfort and no extra stiffness the next morning, a jumping block gets added. <strong>That is where the tendon relearns how to be a spring</strong> — and where most people reinjure themselves, by going from heavy calf raises straight back to intervals. If you are unsure, leave it on "not yet".</p>
  </div>

  <button type="button" class="btn-primary setup-go" id="setupGo">Build my routine →</button>
</div>

<div id="toolWrap" hidden>
{% endraw %}
  {% include "partials/strength-tool.njk" %}
{% raw %}
</div>

<script>
window.ACHILLES_ROUTINE = (function () {

  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------- LIBRARY */
  const L = {
    /* --- rodillo --- */
    rollCalf: { name: "Foam Roll: Calf Belly", sets: 1, reps: "60-90s per side", hold: 60, holdMax: 90, holdSides: 2, tag: "Foam roller",
      cue: "<strong>Stay on the meaty part of the calf. Never roll the tendon</strong> — it is highly innervated tissue and friction there only makes it worse. Sit with the calf on the roller and the other leg crossed on top for load. When you find the spot that complains most, park there instead of sawing back and forth." },

    /* --- isométricos (analgésico + activación) --- */
    isoStep: { name: "Calf Isometric (Step)", sets: 3, reps: "30-45s", hold: 30, holdMax: 45, rest: 60, tag: "Step",
      cue: "Both feet on the step edge, rise onto the toes and hold still at the top. Start here and the rest of the session usually hurts less." },
    isoFlat: { name: "Calf Isometric (Floor)", sets: 3, reps: "30-45s", hold: 30, holdMax: 45, rest: 60, tag: "No equipment",
      cue: "On flat ground, rise onto the toes and hold still at the top. With insertional pain we don't want the heel below foot level." },

    /* --- energía elástica (bloque final, solo si ya no duele) --- */
    pogo: { name: "Ankle Pogos", sets: 3, reps: "20", rest: 60, tag: "No equipment",
      cue: "Small hops in place from the ankle only, knee almost locked. Short, quiet contact. You're not chasing height — you're chasing elastic stiffness." },
    skip: { name: "Low Skipping", sets: 3, reps: "30s", hold: 30, rest: 60, tag: "No equipment",
      cue: "Easy skip in place or traveling. Quick bounce, forefoot contact, no slamming the heel. If the tendon speaks up, the block is over." },
    slHop: { name: "Single-Leg Hops", sets: 3, reps: "10 per side", rest: 60, tag: "No equipment",
      cue: "Small hops in place on one leg, landing on the forefoot with a soft knee. If you wake up stiffer tomorrow than today, it was too soon." },

    /* --- fuerza en casa: excéntricos --- */
    eccStraight: { name: "Straight-Knee Eccentric Calf Lower (Step)", sets: 3, reps: "15 per side", rest: 60, tag: "Step",
      cue: "On the step, rise on both, take the healthy leg off and lower on the working side in 4–5 seconds, heel dropping below. Put the healthy side back to rise. Never go up on the irritated side." },
    eccBent: { name: "Bent-Knee Eccentric Calf Lower (Step)", sets: 3, reps: "15 per side", rest: 60, tag: "Step",
      cue: "Ball of the foot on the step edge, knee bent about 30° the whole time. Rise on two, lower on one in three seconds and let the heel drop. The bent knee shifts load from calf to soleus." },
    eccStraightFlat: { name: "Straight-Knee Eccentric Calf Lower (Floor)", sets: 3, reps: "15 per side", rest: 60, tag: "Flat ground",
      cue: "On the floor, not a step. Rise on both, take the healthy side off and lower on the working side in 4–5 seconds until the heel kisses the floor — then stop. No lower: with insertional pain that extra range crushes the tendon against bone." },
    eccBentFlat: { name: "Bent-Knee Eccentric Calf Lower (Floor)", sets: 3, reps: "15 per side", rest: 60, tag: "Flat ground",
      cue: "On flat ground, knee bent about 30°. Rise on both feet, lower on one in three seconds until the heel kisses the floor — no lower. The bent knee puts the load on the soleus." },

    /* --- fuerza en gimnasio: carga pesada y lenta --- */
    seatedCalf: { name: "Seated Calf Raise", sets: 4, reps: "6-8", rest: 120, tag: "Machine",
      cue: "Seated machine, heavy. 3 seconds up, 3 down. Bent knee: soleus does the work." },
    seatedCalfNeutral: { name: "Seated Calf Raise (Limited Range)", sets: 4, reps: "6-8", rest: 120, tag: "Machine · limited range",
      cue: "Seated machine, heavy, 3 seconds up and 3 down. Stop when the heel reaches platform level — don't drop below. That last bit compresses the insertion." },
    legPressCalf: { name: "Leg-Press Calf Raise", sets: 4, reps: "8-10", rest: 120, tag: "Leg press",
      cue: "Legs almost straight, don't lock the knees back, drive through the forefoot. Slow tempo. With the knee extended, the calf is the main actor." },
    legPressCalfNeutral: { name: "Leg-Press Calf Raise (Limited Range)", sets: 4, reps: "8-10", rest: 120, tag: "Leg press · limited range",
      cue: "On the leg press, slow tempo, don't let the heel drop past the line of the platform. At the insertion, extra range isn't extra stimulus — it's compression." },
    farmerToes: { name: "Weighted Toe Walk", sets: 3, reps: "30s", hold: 30, rest: 90, tag: "Dumbbells",
      cue: "Heavy dumbbells. Walk slowly staying high on the forefoot, heels never dropping. You're training ankle stiffness under load." },

    /* --- pie y tobillo --- */
    towel: { name: "Towel Scrunches", sets: 3, reps: "20 per side", rest: 30, tag: "Towel",
      cue: "Sit barefoot, towel on the floor. Scrunch it toward you with the toes only. A strong foot keeps some of the push-off instead of handing it all to the tendon." },
    shortFoot: { name: "Short Foot", sets: 3, reps: "30s per side", hold: 30, holdSides: 2, rest: 30, tag: "No equipment",
      cue: "Barefoot, foot planted. Draw the ball of the foot toward the heel and lift the arch, without curling the toes or rolling the ankle. It's tiny: when you find it, the arch feels alive." },
    tibRaise: { name: "Toe Raises (Tibialis Anterior)", sets: 3, reps: "20", rest: 30, tag: "Wall for support",
      cue: "Back to the wall, heels a step forward. Lift the toes toward the shins as high as you can and lower slowly. That's the calf's antagonist." },

    /* --- cadera --- */
    hipAbd: { name: "Side-Lying Hip Abduction", sets: 3, reps: "15 per side", rest: 30, tag: "No equipment",
      cue: "Lie on your side, top leg straight, toes pointed slightly toward the floor. Lift slowly. <strong>It should burn on the side of the glute</strong>: if you feel it in the groin, you're rotating the leg." },
    slBridge: { name: "Single-Leg Glute Bridge", sets: 3, reps: "10 per side", rest: 45, tag: "No equipment",
      cue: "One heel close to the glutes, other knee hugged to the chest. Drive through the heel and squeeze the glute at the top. <strong>If you feel it in the low back, tuck the pelvis and lower the height</strong>: the point is to isolate the glute, not let the psoas do the work again." },

    /* --- movilidad (post, en caliente) --- */
    wallStraight: { name: "Wall Calf Stretch (Straight Leg)", sets: 1, reps: "2 min per side", hold: 120, holdSides: 2, tag: "Wall",
      cue: "Hands on the wall, back leg straight, heel pinned. Stretch the calf. Better on warm tissue: stretching a cold, irritated Achilles is the fastest way to flare it." },
    wallBent: { name: "Wall Calf Stretch (Bent Knee)", sets: 1, reps: "2 min per side", hold: 120, holdSides: 2, tag: "Wall",
      cue: "Hands on the wall, back leg behind you, heel pinned. Soften that knee without lifting the heel. The stretch drops into the soleus, closer to the tendon. Torso upright." },
    ankleMob: { name: "Gentle Ankle Mobility", sets: 1, reps: "90s per side", hold: 90, holdSides: 2, tag: "No equipment",
      cue: "Sit, slow big circles, then a held point. No calf stretch on purpose: with insertional pain, end-range dorsiflexion crushes the tendon. Mobility yes, yanking no." }
  };

  /* ------------------------------------------------------------- ROUTINES */
  const ISO      = { mid: ["isoStep"], ins: ["isoFlat"] };
  const ENERGY   = ["pogo", "skip", "slHop"];
  const STRENGTH = {
    "home|mid": ["eccStraight", "eccBent"],
    "home|ins": ["eccStraightFlat", "eccBentFlat"],
    "gym|mid":  ["seatedCalf", "legPressCalf", "farmerToes"],
    "gym|ins":  ["seatedCalfNeutral", "legPressCalfNeutral", "farmerToes"]
  };
  const FOOT     = ["towel", "shortFoot", "tibRaise"];
  const HIP      = ["hipAbd", "slBridge"];
  const MOBILITY = { mid: ["wallStraight", "wallBent"], ins: ["ankleMob"] };

  const WHY = "An injured tendon has disorganised collagen, and the only thing that reorders it is repeated, well-dosed tension. So you don't rest it: you load it, measure the response the next morning, and adjust. Isometrics bring the pain down, slow strength rebuilds the tissue, the foot and hip take work off it, and the jumps — last of all — give it back its ability to be a spring.";

  const RULES =
    "<strong>The traffic-light rules.</strong> Discomfort of <strong>1 to 3 out of 10</strong> during the exercise is fine, as long as it does not build set to set and settles shortly after you finish. " +
    "<strong>Stop</strong> if it goes above 4, if it turns sharp, or if it changes the way you walk.<br><br>" +
    "<strong>The real test is the next morning.</strong> Tendons respond with a delay: if you wake up noticeably stiffer or sorer than your normal day, the load was too much. Drop weight or sets in the next session — do not tough it out.<br><br>" +
    "<strong>Watch for the good-leg cheat.</strong> As fatigue arrives, the body quietly shifts weight onto the healthy leg to get back up. Keep it 50/50, always.";

  const FREQ = {
    home: "<strong>Every day, and twice a day if you can.</strong> This is the classic eccentric protocol: high frequency with little weight, sustained for about 12 weeks.",
    gym: "<strong>Three times a week</strong>, with at least a day between sessions. Heavy slow resistance, also for about 12 weeks."
  };
  const SITE_LABEL = { mid: "Mid-portion pain", ins: "Insertional pain" };
  const PLACE_LABEL = { home: "At home", gym: "At the gym" };

  function build(site, place, roller, stage) {
    const pick = (ids) => ids.map((id) => {
      const e = L[id];
      if (!e) throw new Error("Unknown exercise id: " + id);
      return Object.assign({}, e);
    });

    const phases = [];
    if (roller === "yes") phases.push({ name: "Rolling", exercises: pick(["rollCalf"]) });
    phases.push({ name: "Isometrics", exercises: pick(ISO[site]) });
    // Energy storage goes BEFORE the heavy work, not after: plyometrics belong
    // on fresh legs, and doing them tired is how a tendon that was ready gets
    // re-irritated on the last set.
    if (stage === "yes") phases.push({ name: "Jumps", exercises: pick(ENERGY) });
    phases.push({ name: "Strength", exercises: pick(STRENGTH[place + "|" + site]) });
    phases.push({ name: "Foot and ankle", exercises: pick(FOOT) });
    phases.push({ name: "Hip", exercises: pick(HIP) });
    phases.push({ name: "Mobility", exercises: pick(MOBILITY[site]) });

    return {
      title: "Pain-free Achilles",
      crumb: "Achilles",
      kicker: "Strength",
      subtitle: "Finish each set and tap the button — the only clock here is the rest. The eccentrics are slow on purpose: 4-5 seconds down.",
      context: SITE_LABEL[site] + " · " + PLACE_LABEL[place] + (stage === "yes" ? " · with jumps" : ""),
      why: WHY,
      warning: FREQ[place] + "<br><br>" + RULES,
      doneSub: "Done. Tomorrow morning will tell you whether the dose was right.",
      phases: phases
    };
  }

  return { build: build, library: L };
})();

/* --------------------------------------------------------------- SETUP UI */
(function () {
  const choice = { site: "mid", place: "home", roller: "yes", stage: "no" };

  document.querySelectorAll(".setup-opts").forEach((group) => {
    const axis = group.dataset.axis;
    group.querySelectorAll(".setup-opt").forEach((btn) => {
      btn.addEventListener("click", () => {
        choice[axis] = btn.dataset.val;
        group.querySelectorAll(".setup-opt").forEach((b) =>
          b.setAttribute("aria-pressed", String(b === btn)));
      });
    });
  });

  document.getElementById("setupGo").addEventListener("click", () => {
    window.STRENGTH_DATA = window.ACHILLES_ROUTINE.build(
      choice.site, choice.place, choice.roller, choice.stage);
    document.getElementById("setup").hidden = true;
    document.getElementById("toolWrap").hidden = false;

    const s = document.createElement("script");
    // Fingerprinted engine URL, published by the partial as TP_ENGINE_SRC.
    // A literal path here would be unfingerprinted and therefore pinned in
    // the visitor's browser for a year by Caddy's immutable header on
    // /assets/js/* — the September 8, 2026 defect. Do not inline it back.
    s.src = window.TP_ENGINE_SRC;
    document.body.appendChild(s);

    s.addEventListener("load", () => {
      const back = document.createElement("button");
      back.className = "change-link setup-back";
      back.textContent = "← Change options";
      back.addEventListener("click", () => location.reload());
      document.querySelector(".tool").prepend(back);
    });
  });
})();
</script>
{% endraw %}
